message.content:
{
  "157": {
    "document_id": "157",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The text is only a truncated title referencing a committee markup on venture capital and education bills. It contains no claim of responsibility for any delivered governmental benefit."
  },
  "189": {
    "document_id": "189",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Leahy's hearing statement advocates for cameras in courtrooms, criticizes the Bush administration's secrecy, and notes his cosponsorship of pending bills. Under negative clarification (1) and (2), this is pure position-taking and advocacy for pending measures with no delivered result claimed."
  },
  "744": {
    "document_id": "744",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Allard sent a letter requesting that the Air Force clarify religious expression guidelines at the Academy. Under negative clarification (2), this is advocacy (a letter/meeting request) for action not yet taken, with no delivered benefit claimed."
  },
  "873": {
    "document_id": "873",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release frames the Medicare penalty waiver as a direct response to Landrieu's advocacy: the headline says the 'Administrator finally responds to Sen Landrieu's letter,' the text states the testimony 'responds to urgent calls... made by' Landrieu, and Landrieu says the agency recognized 'the inconsistency our delegation brought to its attention.' Under clarification (2), this juxtaposes the senator's letter with a subsequent favorable executive-branch decision to imply causation, claiming responsibility for a delivered benefit for hurricane-affected Louisiana seniors."
  },
  "964": {
    "document_id": "964",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release announces that Landrieu and Kerry wrote to the Small Business Administration calling for local contracts and faster loss verification in Katrina/Rita rebuilding. Under negative clarification (2), letters and advocacy for measures not yet delivered, with no delivered result claimed, are not credit-claiming."
  },
  "1043": {
    "document_id": "1043",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces a $78,046 federal firefighting grant to an Ohio fire department 'through Sen. DeWine's bill,' notes DeWine 'wrote and passed the FIRE Act,' and says he 'helped secure $650 million' for the program. Under clarification (1) and (3), grant announcements attribute the benefit to the announcing senator, and this also claims credit for enacted legislation and appropriations delivering benefits."
  },
  "1139": {
    "document_id": "1139",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Coleman's statement offers condolences on the death of Rep. Norwood and hopes Congress continues his legacy. Under negative clarification (3), commemorations and condolences are not credit-claiming, and no governmental benefit is claimed."
  },
  "1610": {
    "document_id": "1610",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid's remarks at the CHIP enrollment ceremony praise the bipartisan work of other senators (Baucus, Rockefeller, Grassley, Hatch), criticize the President, and urge him to sign the bill. Reid makes no claim of personal responsibility for a delivered benefit; under negative clarification (1) this is position-taking, and the bill has not even been signed into law."
  },
  "1736": {
    "document_id": "1736",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "While most of the release concerns advocacy for a hearing on pending legislation (S.165), the final paragraph states that Domenici and two House members 'asked' the Agriculture Secretary and President to approve emergency aid for New Mexico, and then notes that USDA announced low-interest emergency loans for 20 New Mexico counties. Under clarification (2), juxtaposing the senator's request with a subsequent favorable executive-branch decision to imply causation constitutes credit-claiming for a delivered benefit."
  },
  "2368": {
    "document_id": "2368",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Craig announces that the VA will expand clinic services for north central Idaho veterans and thanks VA officials for 'their willingness to hear my concerns and - more importantly - respond to the needs of the veterans.' This juxtaposes Craig's advocacy with the favorable VA decision to imply his efforts caused it, fitting clarification (2) on influencing executive-branch decisions (expanded veterans' health services) benefiting constituents."
  },
  "2382": {
    "document_id": "2382",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the release primarily introduces a pending bill (negative clarification (2)), it also states that 'By intervening with the Department of Homeland Security and removing bureaucratic red tape, Sen Landrieu recently ensured that Rayan, a little girl from Iraq with life threatening birth defects was able to come... to the United States for treatment.' This is an explicit claim of having influenced an executive-branch decision to deliver a concrete benefit, satisfying clarification (2) and (5) for a delivered result embedded in a mixed-topic release."
  },
  "2697": {
    "document_id": "2697",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Inouye announces $225,000 in federal funding for coqui frog control on the Big Island and explicitly claims responsibility: after OMB denied Hawaii's request, 'I then went looking and found another federal funding source.' This is a textbook grant/funding announcement credit claim under clarification (1) and the definition of securing federal funds for his state."
  },
  "2827": {
    "document_id": "2827",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lieberman's op-ed argues for continued commitment to the Iraq war and praises President Bush's steadfastness. Under negative clarification (1), this is pure position-taking on foreign policy with no claim of responsibility for any governmental benefit."
  },
  "2895": {
    "document_id": "2895",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Beyond reporting McCaskill's vote, the release states the Senate-passed bill 'contains McCaskill's amendment to provide whistleblower protection and collective bargaining rights for airport screeners.' Under clarification (3), claiming that an amendment the senator authored was included in legislation passed by the chamber and delivers identifiable benefits to a group constitutes credit-claiming, analogous to the codebook's positive example where Senate approval (pre-enactment) sufficed."
  },
  "3038": {
    "document_id": "3038",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Snowe's statement supports the President's nomination of Vice Admiral Allen as Coast Guard Commandant, praising his record and the Coast Guard. She claims no responsibility for the appointment (it is the President's choice) and no delivered benefit; under negative clarification (1) this is position-taking and praise, not credit-claiming."
  },
  "3143": {
    "document_id": "3143",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release reports Senate passage of the Farm Bill and states 'Graham worked to include additional grant programs that could benefit research and training... in South Carolina' and that the bill 'includes a Graham backed provision' providing economic adjustment assistance to textile manufacturers; the SC Agriculture Commissioner thanks Graham for 'securing passage.' Under clarification (3), claiming provisions the senator championed were included in passed legislation delivering identifiable state benefits is credit-claiming."
  },
  "3149": {
    "document_id": "3149",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces Senate passage of the budget resolution and states it includes 'A provision championed by Reed to help establish an affordable housing fund' that will provide grants for affordable housing for low-income families. Under clarification (3), claiming a provision the senator championed was included in passed (Senate-approved) legislation delivering identifiable benefits constitutes credit-claiming, analogous to the codebook's positive example involving chamber approval of a funding request."
  },
  "3817": {
    "document_id": "3817",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Sessions comments on the TVA Board's decision to proceed with a nuclear application, saying he is 'pleased' and 'applauds the Board,' and noting the issue 'has been a priority of mine.' He makes no claim that his actions caused the TVA decision. Under negative clarification (4), welcoming or applauding a favorable government decision without claiming one's actions helped cause it is not credit-claiming."
  },
  "4328": {
    "document_id": "4328",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Alexander and Corker announce that the Department of Transportation decided to support non-stop Jackson-Cincinnati air service through the Essential Air Service program, and the release notes the senators 'wrote a letter in support of Jackson's application to Department of Transportation Secretary Mary Peters.' Under clarification (2), juxtaposing the senators' letter with the subsequent favorable executive-branch decision implies causation, making this a credit claim for a federal benefit to their state."
  },
  "4403": {
    "document_id": "4403",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the release mainly announces cosponsorship of a pending bill (normally negative clarification (2)), it also claims that Domenici 'first authored and offered as legislation in February 2003' the Medicare geographic adjustment provisions enacted in the MMA that put 'rural physicians more on par with urban doctors in terms of Medicare reimbursement levels.' Under clarification (5), claims of past delivered benefits embedded in releases about other topics count, so this release contains a discernible claim of responsibility for enacted provisions delivering benefits to rural New Mexico providers."
  }
}

{
  "50": {
    "document_id": "50",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid's statement is pure criticism of the Bush administration's global warming position. Under negative clarification (1), pure position-taking such as criticism of the administration is not credit-claiming; no delivered benefit or government action attributable to Reid is claimed."
  },
  "57": {
    "document_id": "57",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kennedy's release announces the introduction of the Human Cloning Ban and Stem Cell Research Protection Act. Under negative clarification (2), introductions or cosponsorships of pending bills where no delivered result is claimed are not credit-claiming. The release describes what the bill would do and urges its passage, but claims no enacted or delivered benefit."
  },
  "89": {
    "document_id": "89",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Dorgan announces that the Secretary of Energy designated Bismarck State College as the National Power Plant Operations Technology and Educational Center, and explicitly states he 'authored legislation, which was signed into law, that created the National Center designation.' Under clarification (2) and (3), this is an explicit claim of responsibility for a favorable executive-branch designation and for enacted legislation delivering an identifiable benefit to his state."
  },
  "240": {
    "document_id": "240",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Ensign's statement merely praises the confirmed nominee Chertoff and expresses intent to work with him. Under negative clarification (1), this is pure position-taking/praise with no claim of responsibility for any delivered governmental benefit."
  },
  "453": {
    "document_id": "453",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Nelson's release explains his votes against two Iraq withdrawal resolutions and states his policy preference for measurable goals. Under negative clarification (1), explanations of votes and statements on issues are pure position-taking, not credit-claiming."
  },
  "597": {
    "document_id": "597",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lugar's release describes the presentation of the Dick Lugar Health and Fitness Award to community members at an annual community event. Under negative clarification (3), commemorations, honorary awards, and event announcements are not credit-claiming; no governmental benefit is claimed."
  },
  "656": {
    "document_id": "656",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kerry's statement attacks the Bush administration's port security record following the collapse of the Dubai ports deal. This matches negative example 1 exactly: it is pure criticism/position-taking with no claim of responsibility for any delivered benefit (negative clarification (1))."
  },
  "918": {
    "document_id": "918",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Allard's release merely announces a town meeting in Greeley and lists his committee assignments. This matches negative example 2 exactly: it is an event notice with no claim of any governmental benefit (negative clarification (3))."
  },
  "967": {
    "document_id": "967",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Shelby announces that HHS will release $3,305,732 to Head Start programs in Talladega, Clay, Randolph, and Coosa counties. Under clarification (1), announcements of federal funds awarded to the senator's state or local entities count as credit-claiming because the genre itself attributes the benefit to the announcing senator, even without the word 'secured.'"
  },
  "1021": {
    "document_id": "1021",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Wyden and Smith announce ('GAIN FUNDING APPROVAL') that the Senate Appropriations Committee approved funding for the Pacific Coast Salmon Recovery Fund and Oregon State University ocean research, among other Oregon programs. Under clarification (1) and (3), announcements of appropriations obtained for the senator's state constitute credit-claiming; as in the Talent positive example, approval of the requested funding within the appropriations process suffices even before final enactment."
  },
  "1957": {
    "document_id": "1957",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Dodd and Lieberman announce that Bradley International Airport will receive a $2,250,000 federal grant from the Department of Transportation. Under clarification (1), announcements of federal grants awarded to the senator's state count as credit-claiming because the genre attributes the benefit to the announcing senators."
  },
  "2318": {
    "document_id": "2318",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although framed as a letter urging state action, Durbin explicitly states: 'I helped secure an expedited environmental review from the federal government and facilitated the return of the project to the national priority list for federal funding.' Under clarification (2) (explicit claims of having secured/expedited executive-branch decisions) and clarification (5) (claims of past delivered benefits embedded in releases about other topics), this is credit-claiming."
  },
  "2807": {
    "document_id": "2807",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lincoln 'helped recognize March as Arkansas Women in Agriculture month' and praised women's contributions to agriculture. Under negative clarification (3), commemorations and honorary recognitions are not credit-claiming; no governmental benefit or delivered result is claimed. The references to her Agriculture Committee role advertise position and influence but claim no delivered benefit."
  },
  "3482": {
    "document_id": "3482",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Mikulski and Cardin announce a $294,980 SAFER grant to Allegany County firefighters and note Mikulski's role on the funding subcommittee where she 'fights each year to increase its federal funding.' This matches positive example 2 exactly: a standard grant-announcement credit claim under clarification (1)."
  },
  "3577": {
    "document_id": "3577",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Sarbanes' statement at a hearing criticizes FEMA's handling of flood insurance and its failure to implement reforms. Although he notes he 'worked with Senator Bunning and others on the Committee to formulate and move through' reforms, the point is that FEMA has not implemented them—no concrete achieved result or delivered benefit is claimed. Under negative clarification (6), oversight activity announcements that do not claim concrete achieved results are not credit-claiming."
  },
  "3659": {
    "document_id": "3659",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Clinton welcomes a favorable NIOSH advisory board recommendation for Linde workers but never claims her actions caused it; she urges the Secretary to act and pledges to keep pressing for other workers. This matches negative example 3 exactly: welcoming a favorable decision without claiming causation is advocacy, not credit-claiming (negative clarification (4))."
  },
  "3687": {
    "document_id": "3687",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Bingaman announces that New Mexico State University has been awarded $599,691 in USDA funding for food safety research. Under clarification (1), announcements of federal grants awarded to entities in the senator's state count as credit-claiming because the genre itself attributes the benefit to the announcing senator."
  },
  "4208": {
    "document_id": "4208",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dorgan and Lott announce they will introduce the Media Ownership Act of 2007 to slow the FCC's media ownership rule changes. Under negative clarification (2), introductions of pending bills and advocacy for measures not yet delivered, where no delivered result is claimed, are not credit-claiming."
  },
  "4585": {
    "document_id": "4585",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Schumer announces that two Westchester County fire departments will receive $134,004 in federal Assistance to Firefighters grants, and notes his advocacy for the program ('Schumer has Pushed for Additional Funding'). Under clarification (1), announcements of federal grants awarded to the senator's state or local entities are credit-claiming, with the genre attributing the benefit to the announcing senator."
  },
  "4771": {
    "document_id": "4771",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although framed around a meeting, the release states Clinton 'played a key role along with SMAC in preventing the removal of four C-130 Aircraft' from Stratton Air National Guard base. This matches positive example 3 exactly: a claim of responsibility for a concrete favorable outcome for her state, covered by clarification (2) and (5) (past delivered benefits embedded in releases about other topics)."
  }
}

{
  "42": {
    "document_id": "42",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lott and Collins announce an agreement on provisions to strengthen FEMA that 'will be included in a bill introduced today.' This concerns shaping pending, introduced legislation, not any delivered benefit. Under negative clarification (2) (introductions of and advocacy for pending bills where no delivered result is claimed), this is not credit-claiming; no funds, projects, or enacted outcomes are claimed."
  },
  "714": {
    "document_id": "714",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although much of the release is an informational profile of the U.S. Marshals Service, the senator explicitly states: 'I helped secure more than $3 billion in the fiscal year 2006 Homeland Security appropriations bill for ICE's domestic enforcement efforts, as well as funding for 250 additional criminal investigators.' This is an explicit claim of having secured appropriations, and under clarification (5) claims of past delivered benefits count even when embedded in a release about another topic."
  },
  "794": {
    "document_id": "794",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Much of the release is position-taking (Iraq, taxes, energy), but Bond claims: 'The new federal highway bill, which Bond co-authored, is providing Missouri with much-needed resources, including $1 billion in new money - more than $4 billion in total funding.' This asserts authorship of enacted legislation that is actively delivering identifiable benefits to his state, matching clarification (3) (authored/championed legislation that was passed and delivers identifiable benefits) and clarification (5) (claims embedded in releases on other topics)."
  },
  "805": {
    "document_id": "805",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Shelby, identified as a member of the Appropriations Committee, 'announced that the Department of Homeland Security will release $3,510 to the Homewood Fire Department.' Under clarification (1), announcements of federal grants awarded to the senator's state count as credit-claiming even without the word 'secured,' because the grant-announcement genre itself attributes the benefit to the announcing senator."
  },
  "1257": {
    "document_id": "1257",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "This is a grant announcement layered with explicit credit claims: Parma 'will receive $105,550 as part of his Firefighter Investment and Response Enhancement (FIRE) Act'; 'Senator DeWine wrote and passed the FIRE Act'; and he 'helped secure approximately $650 million for the FIRE Act.' This squarely matches the definition (securing federal grants, taking credit for enacted legislation delivering benefits) and clarification (1) (grant announcements) and (3) (authored legislation delivering benefits)."
  },
  "1325": {
    "document_id": "1325",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dole's floor statement concerns the judicial nominations process, criticizing Democratic filibusters and supporting the 'constitutional option.' It is entirely pure position-taking and criticism of the opposing party, which negative clarification (1) excludes. No governmental benefit is claimed."
  },
  "1749": {
    "document_id": "1749",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Hutchison 'signed on as an original cosponsor of the Veterans Housing Benefits Enhancement Act of 2007,' a bill 'introduced today.' Negative clarification (2) expressly excludes introductions and cosponsorships of pending bills where no delivered result is claimed. The bill's benefits are prospective ('would provide'), so there is no credit claim."
  },
  "1897": {
    "document_id": "1897",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Martinez's statement takes a position on the pending Pombo OCS bill - praising Putnam, objecting to the inadequate buffer, threatening to join a filibuster, and stating conditions for his support. This is pure position-taking on pending legislation under negative clarification (1) and (2); no delivered benefit is claimed."
  },
  "1927": {
    "document_id": "1927",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Snowe 'announced that $8.1 million has been included in the FY 2006 Military Construction, Veterans Affairs Appropriations bill for the Portsmouth Naval Shipyard. This funding was requested by Snowe and Sen. Susan Collins,' and the bill 'passed the Senate today.' She also highlights veterans funding she supported and requested via letter to appropriators. This is an explicit claim of having requested and obtained appropriations for her state - matching the definition and clarifications (1) and (2) (requested funding subsequently included in passed legislation)."
  },
  "2335": {
    "document_id": "2335",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release is primarily an event announcement (a Father's Day essay contest), which alone would be excluded under negative clarification (3). However, it contains embedded credit claims: 'One of Senator Bayh's first pieces of legislation in the Senate was the Responsible Fatherhood Act, parts of which were signed into the law by President Bush earlier this year,' and 'While governor, Senator Bayh also more than doubled the amount of money collected for child support payments.' These are claims of enacted legislation he authored and of past delivered governmental results. Under the definition ('taking credit for enacted legislation') and clarification (5) (claims of past delivered benefits even when embedded in releases about other topics), and the mixed-topic rule (code 1 if a discernible claim of responsibility for a delivered benefit is present), I code this 1. The ambiguity is that the 'parts' enacted are not specified, but the enactment claim is explicit."
  },
  "2869": {
    "document_id": "2869",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Jeffords' statement explains his votes for the Kerry and Levin amendments on Iraq and criticizes the Administration's conduct of the war. This is a vote explanation and pure position-taking, excluded by negative clarification (1). No benefit is claimed."
  },
  "3252": {
    "document_id": "3252",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces as its headline that the 'SENATE ADOPTS NELSON AMENDMENT,' states 'his amendment... passed on a bipartisan vote of 72 to 26,' and Nelson declares 'This is a victory for Nebraska's ranchers, for Nebraska beef.' This is a claim that an amendment he authored was passed and delivers an identifiable benefit (blocking USDA from implementing the Japanese beef rule) to Nebraska cattle producers, matching clarification (3). I considered that the bill still faces conference (not yet enacted), which creates some ambiguity; however, the release's genre is an accomplishment announcement claiming the amendment's adoption as a delivered result, not prospective advocacy, so I code it 1."
  },
  "4022": {
    "document_id": "4022",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kennedy's floor statement introduces 'Medicare for All' legislation and criticizes the Administration and Republicans on health care. Under negative clarification (2), introductions of pending bills and advocacy for measures not yet delivered are excluded; negative clarification (1) excludes the extensive policy criticism. No delivered benefit is claimed."
  },
  "4122": {
    "document_id": "4122",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although this is an oversight/hearing announcement, negative clarification (6) excludes only oversight announcements 'that do not claim concrete achieved results.' This release expressly claims concrete achieved results attributable to the senators' subcommittee: 'Due to the Subcommittee's oversight collections from delinquent Defense contractors skyrocketed by 4,415% from a mere $680,000 in 2003 to $31 million in 2006 alone,' and 'levies on payments to deadbeat federal contractors have jumped 746%, raking in an additional $122 million in back taxes.' Coleman and Levin, as the subcommittee's leaders issuing the release, claim joint responsibility for causing the government to recover funds - a delivered benefit - so it falls outside the exclusion and meets the definition."
  },
  "4184": {
    "document_id": "4184",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kyl 'today co-sponsored legislation' - an amendment to the Budget Resolution calling for 2,000 additional Border Patrol agents. This is cosponsorship of and advocacy for a pending measure; negative clarification (2) excludes introductions/cosponsorships of pending bills where no delivered result is claimed. No agents or funds have been delivered."
  },
  "4207": {
    "document_id": "4207",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Cornyn's release centers on a letter to Secretary Chertoff urging consideration of the IBWC levee proposal, plus a hosted roundtable and requests for appropriations - all advocacy for measures not yet delivered, excluded by negative clarification (2) (letters, meetings, advocacy where no delivered result is claimed). The release says Cornyn 'has made several efforts... to see that federal funding is increased' - efforts, not secured funds. He did add an amendment requiring an IBWC report to Congress, but a reporting requirement is procedural, not a tangible delivered benefit. No favorable decision or funding is claimed as delivered."
  },
  "4295": {
    "document_id": "4295",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the release announces the introduction of new FMAP legislation (itself excluded advocacy), it contains an embedded claim of a past delivered benefit: 'Senator Lisa Murkowski in 2003 proposed a permanent formula change... she helped win a two-year change in the Economic Growth Reconciliation Act... that provided the state with an additional $34.11 million above the traditional Medicaid formula amount.' Under clarification (5), claims of past delivered benefits count even when embedded in releases about other topics, and this matches clarification (3) (a provision she championed was included in enacted legislation and delivered identifiable benefits to Alaska)."
  },
  "4508": {
    "document_id": "4508",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Lott announces FEMA is moving ~300 travel trailers and 100 mobile homes to South Mississippi for Katrina victims, and the release states: 'The announcement follows the Senator's Monday appeal to President Bush to authorize the immediate transport of available trailers staged outside the Katrina disaster area.' This juxtaposition of the senator's request with the subsequent favorable executive decision to imply causation is precisely what clarification (2) includes as credit-claiming (claims of having secured, requested, or expedited executive-branch decisions benefiting constituents)."
  },
  "4677": {
    "document_id": "4677",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Hutchison's release describes a subcommittee hearing at which she asked questions, received assurances about BRAC implementation, and 'expressed her support' for the Army's budget request. She says she 'has supported increasing the Army's end strength and am encouraged to see $18 million in the emergency supplemental for facilities at Fort Hood.' Being 'encouraged to see' funding is welcoming a development without claiming her actions caused it (negative clarification (4)), and the hearing questions are oversight activity without a claim of achieved results (negative clarification (6)). No discernible claim of responsibility for a delivered benefit."
  },
  "4774": {
    "document_id": "4774",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release's purpose is prospective advocacy: Bayh, Durbin, and Landrieu 'sent letters' urging conferees to retain the Patriot Penalty provision, emphasizing that it 'must remain in the conference version that goes before the President's desk for signature in order to become law.' Negative clarification (2) excludes letters and other advocacy for measures not yet delivered. The release does note the Senate earlier 'unanimously approved an amendment offered by Senators Bayh and Durbin,' which creates ambiguity (compare clarification (3)); however, the release itself frames the benefit as undelivered and contingent, and the claim of Senate approval serves only as background to the appeal, so I code it 0."
  }
}
